# Redstone cell library, in SIS Genlib format (the format Yosys's
# `abc -genlib <file>` pass reads: see `help abc` in Yosys, and ABC's own
# `mio.c` parser). This is the frontend's cost model: it tells ABC's
# technology mapper what our cells actually cost in redstone, in the same
# units the rest of this codebase already measures itself in, so that ABC's
# own logic-optimisation choices land in the same place a human designing for
# this hardware would land.
#
# Every number below is derived, not invented -- see the comment above each
# GATE for its source. Nothing here was tuned to make a particular circuit
# come out smaller.
#
# NOR1/NOR2/NOR3/BUF/OR2/OR3's AREA and delay fields are not just derived by hand:
# `src/compile/topology.rs`'s `genlib_cost` computes the same numbers straight
# from that module's own `Library` (which cell type maps to which entry is
# `topology::gate_kind_for_yosys_cell`'s table; `src/frontend/yosys_json.rs`
# consults both, instead of re-deciding what a Yosys cell type is on its own).
# `src/frontend/mod.rs`'s `genlib_numbers_match_what_the_topology_library_derives`
# test asserts this file's numbers agree with that derivation, so the two
# cannot silently drift apart. This file itself stays hand-written rather than
# generated -- it is SIS Genlib syntax ABC parses, and a byte-identical static
# file is what guarantees ABC's own mapping decisions cannot shift as a side
# effect of a refactor here.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why NOR, and why fan-in barely matters
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `place_nor_gate` (src/compile/mod.rs) is the one cell this whole project
# builds circuits out of: redstone dust merging into a block with a torch on
# it. Its *delay* is one redstone torch's propagation delay, completely
# independent of how many inputs feed the merge -- `TORCH_DELAY_GAME_TICKS`
# (src/redstone/simulator/component.rs) is a flat 2 game ticks whether 1, 2,
# or 3 signals merge into the same charge. That is the opposite of CMOS,
# where more fan-in means a slower, larger gate, and it is exactly why a
# synthesiser tuned for CMOS (which decomposes aggressively into small gates)
# optimises in the wrong direction here: decomposing into more, narrower NOR
# gates only adds gate delay and wiring, for zero improvement in per-gate
# delay.
#
# Area is a different story: an NOR cell's footprint on the ground plane
# *does* grow with its fan-in, because each extra input needs its own socket
# against the shared support block (`place_nor_gate`'s `INPUT_DIRECTIONS`:
# West, East, South -- North is reserved for the output torch). The AREA
# figure below is exactly that footprint -- the compiled ground-plan
# rectangle's X*Z, in blocks -- for a gate with 1, 2, and 3 inputs
# respectively, read straight off `place_nor_gate`'s own bounding-box
# computation (verified by placing one of each into a scratch `World` and
# reading back `NorCell.size`):
#
#   inputs   NorCell.size (x,y,z)   ground footprint (x * z)
#   1        (2, 1, 3)              6
#   2        (3, 1, 3)              9
#   3        (3, 1, 4)              12
#
# (These shrank from an earlier revision of this library -- 9/15/25 -- when
# `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-08-3d-codesign.md`'s cell dissolution
# removed a NOR gate's separate per-input support block and merge dust: a
# gate is now just one support block plus its output torch, with every
# input's own route terminating directly against a free face of that same
# block. The *ratios* between fan-in counts shifted too (2.78x -> 2.0x for
# 3-input vs. 1-input), which is a genlib change ABC's technology mapper sees,
# not merely a cosmetic renumbering -- see the note on `NOR3` below.)
#
# So fan-in is cheap but not free -- exactly the asymmetry this project's
# whole cost model rests on, now expressed in ABC's own terms instead of
# CMOS's.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The cells
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# $__ZERO / $__ONE: constant drivers. ABC's newer "&nf" mapper (the one
# `abc -genlib` invokes by default -- see `help abc`'s default-script list)
# requires a library to define these before it will run at all; a genlib
# without them fails technology mapping outright, even for designs that
# never need a constant. This library still has no way to *realize* a true
# constant driver in redstone (there is no "always-on" cell), so the Rust
# reader (`src/frontend/yosys_json.rs`) treats either one actually appearing
# in a mapped netlist as an unsupported construct and fails loudly, rather
# than silently inventing a value.
GATE $__ZERO 0 Y=CONST0;
GATE $__ONE 0 Y=CONST1;

# BUF: a pass-through. ABC's mapper also needs a buffer cell to exist in the
# library for internal bookkeeping (e.g. reconciling a wire that a
# `assign out = in;` turns into with no logic in between at all) -- without
# one, mapping fails the same way it does without the constants above. There
# is no native "wire-only" primitive in redstone either: every real signal
# path here ends in a torch. The Rust reader realizes a BUF as two chained
# 1-input NOR cells (NOT(NOT(x)) == x), so its cost here is honestly
# `2 * NOR1`: area 2*6 = 12, delay 2*2 = 4 game ticks.
GATE BUF 12 Y=A;
PIN * NONINV 1 9999 4 0 4 0

# NOR1 (== an inverter): area 6 from the table above; delay is one torch,
# `TORCH_DELAY_GAME_TICKS` = 2 game ticks, in the "rise/fall block delay"
# fields (the "fanout delay" fields are 0 -- this library models delay as a
# pure per-gate constant, since wire delay is a placement/routing cost this
# genlib deliberately has no visibility into; see the top-level task comment
# for why that split is the right one here).
GATE NOR1 6 Y=!A;
PIN * INV 1 9999 2 0 2 0

# NOR2: area 9 from the table above. Same delay as every other NOR here --
# one torch, independent of fan-in.
GATE NOR2 9 Y=!(A+B);
PIN * INV 1 9999 2 0 2 0

# NOR3: area 12 from the table above -- the hardware maximum fan-in
# (`place_nor_gate` only has three input faces free; the fourth is the
# output). Same delay as NOR1/NOR2. ABC therefore has no delay incentive at
# all to prefer a narrower gate, and only a mild area incentive (12 for one
# NOR3 vs. paying for two separate gates plus the wire between them to get
# the same fan-in another way) -- which is exactly the redstone trade-off,
# not the CMOS one.
GATE NOR3 12 Y=!(A+B+C);
PIN * INV 1 9999 2 0 2 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# OR2 / OR3: a wire merge, not a gate
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Redstone dust joining is already the maximum-of-sources operation an OR
# is -- see docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-08-gate-types-and-wired-or.md,
# "In redstone an OR is free". `compile` realizes an `is_merge` gate as
# exactly that: no support block, no torch, nothing at the gate's own
# location but the point where its declared inputs' own dust runs are
# allowed to physically touch (`place_merge_gate`, `src/compile/mod.rs`).
#
# But "no torch" is not "no cell". `place_merge_gate` returns a real
# `NorCell` with a real `size`, computed by the same bounding-box walk over
# the same `INPUT_DIRECTIONS` sockets `place_nor_gate` uses, and the
# floorplanner hands it a row and a slot exactly as it hands one to a NOR
# (`compute_asap_levels` levelises merge and non-merge gates identically).
# AREA in this file has only ever meant that reserved ground plan -- the
# compiled rectangle's X*Z -- so a merge's area is its own rectangle, read
# off `place_merge_gate` the same way NOR1/NOR2/NOR3's are read off
# `place_nor_gate`:
#
#   inputs   NorCell.size (x,y,z)   ground footprint (x * z)   a NOR's, for comparison
#   2        (3, 1, 2)              6                          9
#   3        (3, 1, 3)              9                          12
#
# A merge is one row of Z cheaper than a NOR of the same fan-in, and that
# is the whole difference: its output socket sits one hop north of the
# junction, where a NOR's sits two hops north of its support (torch, then
# socket). `topology::merge_footprint_area` holds the same table, and
# `topology::tests::every_cell_footprint_matches_what_the_placer_actually_reserves`
# checks both tables against what the placer really writes into a scratch
# `World`, so neither can drift from `place_merge_gate`/`place_nor_gate`.
#
# The DELAY fields stay 0, and that number is honest for *both* ways
# `compile` can terminate a branch into a merge:
#
#   - A **private** branch (its source drives nothing besides this merge)
#     joins as bare dust: no repeater, no gate, and the real, measured
#     settle time contribution is 0 game ticks (see the referenced spec's
#     verification, and `tests/cell_type_costs.rs`'s
#     `or_is_a_free_wire_merge_when_nothing_else_shares_the_branch`).
#   - A **shared** branch (its source also feeds something else) needs a
#     repeater so backflow through the merge cannot corrupt that other
#     consumer. But that repeater is not a new cost OR introduces: it is
#     the exact same mandatory route-terminating repeater every socket in
#     this compiler already pays unconditionally, NOR1/NOR2/NOR3's own
#     input sockets included (`src/compile/mod.rs`'s module doc comment:
#     "a route always ends in a repeater facing the next gate's support
#     block"). Choosing a merge over a NOR-decomposition never adds that
#     repeater on top of some cheaper baseline route -- it is simply not
#     the one special case (a bare join) that gets to omit it.
#
# So the isolated branch's repeater is exactly as much "this gate's own
# cost" as a plain NOR's own input-route repeater is: not at all. Neither
# realisation puts a torch on the path, and both reserve the identical
# rectangle (`place_merge_gate` does not know which one it is building), so
# the one pair of numbers below prices both honestly. See
# `src/compile/topology.rs`'s `genlib_cost`, "OR: one number, honestly
# standing for two realisations", for the full derivation, and
# `frontend::tests::genlib_numbers_match_what_the_topology_library_derives`
# for the test that keeps this file and that derivation from drifting
# apart, across *every* registered entry for `Or(2)`/`Or(3)` -- not just
# the first one -- so "one number for both" is proven, not asserted.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why not AREA 0: measured, not argued
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# These lines were introduced at AREA 0, on the reasoning that this genlib
# prices gate bodies, has never priced wire for any cell, and a merge is
# entirely wire. Every step of that is true except the conclusion -- it
# skips the step above, that a merge is still a cell with a reserved
# footprint. And a cell priced at zero is one ABC will buy without limit.
# It did: the Verilog-derived seven-segment decoder got *bigger*, from
# 37 gates / 8130 blocks (OR absent from the library) to 50 / 10668.
#
# Re-measured through the real compiler -- `cargo test --release --test
# verilog_frontend the_verilog_seven_segment`, one full recompile per row,
# OR3 held at 1.5x OR2 (the footprint ratio above), every other cell
# untouched. Columns are the mapped decoder's gates / non-air blocks /
# worst-case settle in game ticks:
#
#   OR2  OR3 | delay 0        | delay 1        | delay 2        | delay 3
#   ---------+----------------+----------------+----------------+---------------
#     1    2 | 50 10668  80   | 44 10556  74   |      (a)       | 40 10254  67
#     2    3 | 44  9130  72   | 41  9372  68   | 42  9170  68   |      (a)
#     3    4 | 34  8454  84   | 34  8060  66   | 34  7952  60   | 33  9050  62
#     4    6 | 31  7888  82   | 31  7572  78   | 31  7162  60   | 30  8730  64
#     5    7 | 31  7888  82   | 31  7572  78   | 30  7578  62   | 30  8730  64
#     6    9 | 31  7888  82   | 31  7572  78   |      (a)       | 30  8730  64   <- derived
#     7   10 | 31  7888  82   | 31  7572  78   |      (a)       | 30  8730  64
#     8   12 | 31  8396  76   | 31  8478  74   |      (a)       | 29  8212  63
#     9   13 |      (a)       | 31  8880  78   | 30  8816  76   | 29  8212  63
#    12   18 | 31 10278  82   | 31  9036  69   | 30  8816  76   |      (a)
#    18   27 | 32  8546  75   | 32  9546  65   | 35  8472  66   | 35  8646  68
#   1000 1000| 37  8130  70   -- priced out of reach; ABC uses no OR at all
#
#   (a) the run compiled and matched its truth table, but tripped
#       `tests/verilog_frontend.rs`'s exactness assertion on the
#       critical-path settle *model* (it under-predicts on some
#       merge-heavy netlists). A pre-existing limitation of
#       `timing::summarize_worst_case`, not of this price list -- it is
#       recorded here rather than worked around, and the derived row is
#       not one of them.
#
# Two things this table settles, neither of which was knowable by argument:
#
# 1. **The area price is the whole story for size.** Priced out of reach,
#    the decoder is exactly the 37/8130/70 it was before OR existed -- so
#    the merge routing added in the same change is size-neutral, and the
#    entire 37 -> 50 gate regression was AREA 0. At the derived price it is
#    31 gates / 7888 blocks: better than the pre-OR decoder on both, and
#    the 4..7 plateau means the derived 6 is not perched on a knife edge.
#
# 2. **Settle does not follow.** At the honest delay of 0 no area price in
#    this table reaches the pre-OR 70 ticks -- the best is 75, and the
#    derived row is 82. This is not a mispricing, it is what a merge costs
#    in this floorplan: the critical path goes from 5 gates + 28 repeaters
#    (no OR, 70 ticks) to 3 gates + 36 repeaters (derived, 82). Merges
#    genuinely shorten the logic chain, by two whole gates; they lengthen
#    the wire more than that saves, because a merge still occupies a level
#    and its branches have to reach it. Rows that do beat 70 ticks all buy
#    it by telling ABC an OR has a *gate delay* it does not have -- there
#    is no torch on the path through a merge, so those are fabricated
#    numbers and none of them is used here. Making settle follow size is a
#    floorplanning problem (the router's own cost model), not a genlib one.
#
# Yosys/ABC's own naming: a design's `$_OR_` cells (Yosys's pre-mapping,
# generic name) never appear in a mapped netlist -- `abc -genlib` renames
# every cell it maps to whichever GATE line it chose, so a mapped OR is
# always literally `OR2` or `OR3` here, the same way a mapped NOR is
# `NOR2`/`NOR3`, never `$_NOR_`.
GATE OR2 6 Y=(A+B);
PIN * NONINV 1 9999 0 0 0 0

GATE OR3 9 Y=(A+B+C);
PIN * NONINV 1 9999 0 0 0 0
